ition:** INDEPENDENT\n**Automated operational score:** 95/100 — LOW — APPROVE\n*This tool is for demonstration purposes only.*\n\n### Summary\nThe AI reviewer reported 1 confirmed actionable finding(s).\n\n### Findings\n- **LOW · SECURITY_VULNERABILITY · Inbound AllowHTTP NSG rule comment contradicts the configured source address prefix**\n  - Category rationale: The inline comment on the AllowHTTP rule states 'This rule permits inbound TCP/80 from any IPv4 address', but the actual Terraform value is source_address_prefix = \"203.0.113.10/32\". The comment contradicts the code. If an operator relies on the comment description for a change review, incident response, or audit, they may believe the rule is broader (any source) than it actually is, or vice versa. Conversely, if the intent was truly any-source and the comment is authoritative, then the configured prefix is wrong and the rule is more restrictive than intended. Either way, a material discrepancy between the stated intent and the configured value is a security-relevant defect: it undermines correct change review and could mask an error in either direction.\n  - Evidence: azurerm_network_security_group.nsg, security_rule 'AllowHTTP': source_address_prefix = \"203.0.113.10/32\" with inline comment '# This rule permits inbound TCP/80 from any IPv4 address' and preceding comment '# TC04: Deliberately public HTTP access for security testing only.'\n  - Analysis: The AllowHTTP security rule (priority 302, inbound, TCP/80) has an inline comment that reads 'This rule permits inbound TCP/80 from any IPv4 address', but source_address_prefix is set to '203.0.113.10/32', which restricts access to a single host. The TC04 comment above the rule states 'Deliberately public HTTP access for security testing only', which is consistent with any-source access, not a /32. The effective behaviour and the stated intent are in direct conflict. If the /32 is correct, the comments are misleading and must be corrected so that reviewers and operators understand the actual scope. If any-source was intended, the current rule is incorrectly restrictive, potentially breaking the stated security-testing purpose.\n  - Impact: Low direct exploitability because the narrower /32 actually reduces attack surface compared to any-source. However, if the intent is any-source and the /32 is a typo or misconfiguration, the security-test scenario fails silently. In either case, misleading infrastructure comments degrade the integrity of code review, change management, and incident response.\n  - Recommendation: Align the comment with the configured value. If 203.0.113.10/32 is the correct and intentional source restriction, update the inline comment to accurately describe it (e.g., 'permits inbound TCP/80 from 203.0.113.10 only') and adjust the TC04 comment to remove the 'publicly' framing. If any-source access was intended for the security-testing scenario, correct source_address_prefix to \"*\" or \"Internet\" and validate that this is acceptable under SEC-001 with explicit justification recorded.\n\n### Recommendation\nReview and remediate the confirmed findings above.\n","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/abdulwaheed384/ai-pr-review/issues/comments/6147794093/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":{"id":15368,"client_id":"Iv1.05c79e9ad1f6bdfa","slug":"github-actions","node_id":"MDM6QXBwMTUzNjg=","owner":{"login":"github","id":9919,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjk5MTk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9919?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github","html_url":"https://github.com/github","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/us